  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is leveraging its fisheries expertise to strengthen the skills and capacity of Southeast Asian fisheries agencies to combat illegal, unreported and unregulated (IUU) fishing. In 2024, the flagship Monitoring, Control and Surveillance (MCS) course was conducted for the second time at Nha Trang University in Vietnam. The training program is the first of its kind in Southeast Asia and was co-designed and delivered by the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, the Australian Fisheries Management Authority and the Commonwealth Scientific and Industrial Research Organisation with a broad curriculum covering both theoretical and practical elements of fisheries MCS. In 2024, 44 per cent of attendees were female fisheries officers, a significant increase from the estimated 10 per cent female participation in 2023. Separately, demand-driven, tailored workshops were also delivered in Cambodia, Vietnam and Thailand, further demonstrating Australia&#x2019;s commitment and ability to respond to regional training needs as they are identified.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is strengthening capacity in fisheries agencies through the Combating Illegal, Unreported and Unregulated (IUU) Fishing and Promoting Sustainable Fisheries in Southeast Asia program. Improving gender inclusivity and women&#x2019;s leadership in fisheries management is key outcome for the program, which facilitated a 2024 Women in Fisheries workshop in Thailand. Of the 65 participants, most were female but a number of male gender inclusivity champions also attended. Participants indicated the workshop provided opportunity to share &#x2018;stories and experiences, and challenges women face in the sector&#x2019;, recognise participants&#x2019; ongoing commitment to &#x2018;promote cooperation through a regional network&#x2019;, and acknowledge the importance of &#x2018;mainstreaming gender concepts into workplans to improve understanding within the agency and to promote the importance of the contribution of women in combatting IUU Fishing&#x2019;. Participants also spoke of returning to their workplace committed to &#x2018;improving policy design and taking steps to implement effective gender inclusion in fisheries management&#x2019; and &#x2018;looking into capacity development to raise awareness to attract more women into Monitoring, Control and Surveillance roles, and to encourage women to participate in public forums on IUU fishing to demonstrate their leadership&#x2019;. Establishing a regional Women in Fisheries Network has been added to the program&#x2019;s workplan following the workshop.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
